    "html": "<h1>FAQ</h1>\n<h2>Is it open source?</h2>\n<p>MIT licensed. v1.1.1 is published on npm; the source repository opens at the public launch.</p>\n<h2>Which agents work with it?</h2>\n<p>OpenCode, Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Gemini CLI, Copilot CLI and VS Code Copilot — through the open standards: AGENTS.md, SKILL.md and MCP.</p>\n<h2>Does it need a server?</h2>\n<p>No. The kit is a local native binary; the artifacts live in your own git.</p>\n<h2>What is a RN?</h2>\n<p>A numbered business rule — the versioned agreement between you and your client. Example: <code>RN-007 — corrida só pode ser aceita pelo motorista dentro de 60 segundos</code>. Rules are written in plain language, approved by humans, and cited as evidence by the code that implements them.</p>\n<h2>How is this different from prompt-only tools?</h2>\n<p>The truth is not the prompt — it is the versioned state. A deterministic binary validates it; agents only propose. When code and documentation diverge, the kit names the exact rule and the exact file.</p>\n",
